1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,343
About sixty-five
billion neutrinos

2
00:00:02,357 --> 00:00:04,227
from the Sun pass
through your little

3
00:00:04,227 --> 00:00:05,842
fingernail every second,

4
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:08,010
and almost none of
them touch anything.

5
00:00:08,430 --> 00:00:09,670
So how do you catch one?

6
00:00:10,750 --> 00:00:13,113
This year's Nobel
Prize in Physics went

7
00:00:13,154 --> 00:00:15,401
to Francis Halzen, for decisive

8
00:00:15,455 --> 00:00:18,354
contributions to
IceCube, a telescope

9
00:00:18,402 --> 00:00:21,133
made of Antarctic
ice, and the discovery

10
00:00:21,184 --> 00:00:23,510
of high-energy
neutrinos from space.

11
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:26,480
Four steps, and three
guesses for you.

12
00:00:27,380 --> 00:00:28,970
Step one: the ghost particle.

13
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:31,508
Guess: a high-energy neutrino

14
00:00:31,550 --> 00:00:33,660
crosses a whole
kilometre of ice.

15
00:00:33,950 --> 00:00:35,220
What are the odds it hits

16
00:00:35,220 --> 00:00:36,310
anything on the way?

17
00:00:40,330 --> 00:00:41,357
In our toy model,

18
00:00:41,480 --> 00:00:43,670
about one in
sixty-five thousand.

19
00:00:44,090 --> 00:00:45,933
So the detector had to be huge.

20
00:00:46,840 --> 00:00:48,550
Step two: the telescope.

21
00:00:48,950 --> 00:00:51,412
In nineteen eighty-eight,
Halzen and John

22
00:00:51,468 --> 00:00:53,890
Learned proposed
using the ice itself.

23
00:00:54,245 --> 00:00:56,270
Halzen then led the
team that built it.

24
00:00:58,460 --> 00:01:00,603
When one does hit,
it makes a charged

25
00:01:00,610 --> 00:01:03,210
particle faster than
light travels in ice.

26
00:01:03,640 --> 00:01:05,440
It leaves a cone of blue light,

27
00:01:05,770 --> 00:01:07,690
and each sensor
times its arrival.

28
00:01:09,630 --> 00:01:11,983
Step three: our toy telescope.

29
00:01:12,066 --> 00:01:13,586
From the light's timing alone,

30
00:01:13,586 --> 00:01:15,350
it finds the
particle's direction.

31
00:01:15,910 --> 00:01:17,773
Neutrinos fly straight, so that

32
00:01:17,828 --> 00:01:19,648
points back to where
they came from.

33
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,655
Guess: same ice,
strings twice as

34
00:01:22,727 --> 00:01:24,720
far apart, so a quarter as many.

35
00:01:25,090 --> 00:01:26,528
How much worse does it aim?

36
00:01:30,580 --> 00:01:32,577
In our toy model,
more than five times

37
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:34,800
worse: fewer
strings, less light.

38
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:37,880
Trend only, not
IceCube's real aim.

39
00:01:38,740 --> 00:01:40,280
Step four: what it found.

40
00:01:40,730 --> 00:01:43,024
In twenty thirteen,
IceCube reported

41
00:01:43,060 --> 00:01:44,903
the first evidence
of high-energy

42
00:01:44,951 --> 00:01:46,790
neutrinos from outer space.

43
00:01:47,250 --> 00:01:49,590
In twenty sixteen, it
caught this shower,

44
00:01:49,880 --> 00:01:51,600
replayed from the real data.

45
00:01:52,530 --> 00:01:54,304
IceCube records about a hundred

46
00:01:54,340 --> 00:01:55,950
thousand neutrinos a year.

47
00:01:56,370 --> 00:01:58,009
Guess: how many of them

48
00:01:58,050 --> 00:01:59,302
come from outer space?

49
00:02:03,390 --> 00:02:06,010
About a hundred, the
Nobel Committee says.

50
00:02:06,345 --> 00:02:07,615
Most of the rest are made

51
00:02:07,615 --> 00:02:08,830
in our own atmosphere.

52
00:02:09,700 --> 00:02:11,510
The prize honours
that discovery:

53
00:02:11,840 --> 00:02:14,010
high-energy
neutrinos from space.

54
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:16,560
Which objects make
them is still open:

55
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:19,250
one nearby galaxy
shows evidence,

56
00:02:19,420 --> 00:02:20,381
not yet proof.

57
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:22,570
So how do you catch one?

58
00:02:22,960 --> 00:02:24,661
With a cubic kilometre of ice,

59
00:02:24,824 --> 00:02:25,710
still watching.

60
00:02:26,580 --> 00:02:28,875
Made with showtime,
an open-source

61
00:02:28,922 --> 00:02:30,821
video studio for coding agents.
